@@ -1184,6 +1186,33 @@ These limits are approximate, so `vmagent` can underflow or overflow them by a s
 
 See also [cardinality explorer docs](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/single-server-victoriametrics/#cardinality-explorer).
 
+## Limiting the number of scrape targets per job
+
+Service discovery mechanisms (Kubernetes, Consul, cloud APIs, file-based discovery, etc.) sometimes misbehave,
+for example because of a broken filter or an overly broad query on the discovery side, and suddenly return
+a huge number of targets for a single `job_name`. Since `vmagent` scrapes all discovered targets by default,
+this can spike memory and CPU usage and degrade scraping for every other job running on the same `vmagent` instance.
+
+By default, `vmagent` doesn't limit the number of simultaneously scraped targets for a single job.
+The limit can be enforced in the following places:
+
+* Via `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag. This limit is applied individually
+  to every `job_name` defined in the file pointed to by `-promscrape.config`.
+* Via `max_scrape_targets` config option at [scrape_config](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs) section.
+  The `max_scrape_targets` option allows overriding the `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` on a per-`scrape_config` basis.
+  If `max_scrape_targets` is set to `0`, then the limit isn't applied to the given `scrape_config`,
+  even if the `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag is set.
+
+The limit is applied to the total number of targets discovered for the given `job_name`, regardless of which
+service discovery mechanism (or combination of mechanisms) produced them. When the number of discovered targets
+for a job exceeds the configured limit, `vmagent` keeps scraping only the first N targets for that job,
+while the rest are dropped instead of crashing or rejecting the whole job.
+
+Targets dropped because of this limit are shown with the `max_scrape_targets` drop reason at the
+[`/targets` and `/api/v1/targets`](#monitoring) pages, alongside the already-scraped targets for the job.
+This makes it easy to notice, from the same pages used for everyday scrape target troubleshooting, which jobs
+are currently hitting the limit and roughly how many targets are being excluded because of it.
+
 ## Monitoring
 
 `vmagent` exports various metrics in Prometheus exposition format at `http://vmagent-host:8429/metrics` page.
